      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The Pro12Ala polymorphism in the peroxisome Proliferator-activated receptor-gamma2 PPARγ2) gene that account for metabolic dysfunction in women with polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) remain elusive."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "To explore the association between PPARγ2 gene pro12ala polymorphism and the metabolic characteristics in Chinese women with PCOS."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "PPARγ2 gene Pro12Ala polymorphism was assayed by PCR/RFLP methods in 120 Chinese women with PCOS and 118 normal subjects. All subjects were examined by anthropometry, lipid profile, sex hormone, oral glucose tolerance tests and insulin tolerance tests."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "In PCOS patients, women with the non-Pro/Pro genotypes of the PPARγ2 gene Pro12Ala polymorphism showed statistically significantly higher fasting triglycerides (TG) levels and WHR value than those with the Pro/Pro genotype (P=.006 for both). There was no significant difference with PPARγ2 Pro12Ala polymorphism distributions between Chinese Han women with PCOS and controls."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "PPARγ2 gene Pro12Ala polymorphism was not supposed to be susceptible genes in PCOS. However, in PCOS patients, the PPAR-gamma Pro12Ala polymorphism may modulate the concentrations of serum fasting TG levels and fat-deposition in abdomen, respectively."
        }
      ]},
